# API lookup

The spec at `API_SPEC` (`.claude/harness.conf`) is generated from the app — the
same models the server serialises — so it is the route shapes' only source of
truth. A hand-written route reference beside it is a regression.

**Never read the spec.** Every command below prints plain text — one route
costs ~100–250 tokens.

```bash
S=.claude/skills/api-lookup/api-lookup.py

python3 $S list                                  # every route (+ x-auth if present)
python3 $S list --prefix /api/admin --method get
python3 $S list --tag users --auth admin
python3 $S show GET /api/users/{id}              # one route, $refs expanded
python3 $S search email                          # field or schema -> routes
python3 $S schema UserOut                        # one component schema
```

## Auth

If the generator writes an `x-auth` extension on each operation — derived from
the route's real auth dependency, not a comment — `list` and `show` print it and
`--auth` filters on it. Deriving it from the dependency tree is what keeps it
from drifting; a hand-maintained value is worse than none. {{What each x-auth
value means here, and any scoping the router enforces that x-auth cannot show —
or delete this section.}}

## Writing

Read-only. To change a shape, change the {{response model / route}} in
{{path}}, then regenerate with {{command}} and move its client mirror
({{path}}) in the same change.

## Limits

Shapes, status codes and auth only. Behaviour OpenAPI cannot express —
idempotency, background work, why a route 404s instead of 403ing — lives in the
route code. Read that for *why*; use this for *what the wire carries*.

The same pattern extends to the database: dump tables, columns, keys and access
policies to a generated JSON (from the models plus a replay of the migrations),
and add `tables` / `table` / `column` commands here.
